{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Use $e(t)=e^{2\pi it}$ and the <Fourier transform> convention $\widehat f(\xi)=\int_{\mathbb R}f(x)e(-x\xi)\,dx$, initially for an <integrable function>. For the interval <indicator function>, direct integration gives
$$
\widehat{1_{[0,1]}}(\xi)=\begin{cases}(1-e(-\xi))/(2\pi i\xi),&\xi\ne0,\\1,&\xi=0.\end{cases}
$$
The integral bound is one, and the displayed numerator has modulus at most two. By the <convolution theorem>, the <Fourier transform> of the $m$-fold <convolution> is its $m$th power. Thus the <Fourier decay of repeated interval convolutions> gives
$$
\boxed{|\widehat f(\xi)|\le\min(1,(\pi|\xi|)^{-m})\ll\min(1,|\xi|^{-m}).}
$$
At zero, interpret the second term of the minimum as infinity.

The <Schwartz space> $\mathcal S(\mathbb R)$ consists of <smooth functions> for which every <Schwartz seminorm> $\sup_x|x^a f^{(b)}(x)|$ is finite, for nonnegative integers $a,b$. <Differentiation under the integral sign> and <integration by parts> give
$$
\widehat f^{(b)}(\xi)=\widehat{(-2\pi ix)^bf}(\xi),\qquad
(2\pi i\xi)^a\widehat f^{(b)}(\xi)=\widehat{\frac{d^a}{dx^a}\bigl((-2\pi ix)^bf(x)\bigr)}(\xi).
$$
All differentiated integrands are <integrable>, and their boundary terms vanish because of rapid decay. The last transform is bounded by its integrand's $L^1$ <norm>. The same argument applies at every derivative order, so $\widehat f\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R)$.

For $f\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R)$, the <Poisson summation formula> is
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}f(n)=\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z}\widehat f(k).}
$$
To prove it, form the <periodization of a Schwartz function> $F(x)=\sum_n f(x+n)$. Rapid decay gives <uniform convergence> of this series and of every differentiated series on $[0,1]$, so $F$ is a smooth <periodic function>. Its <Fourier coefficients> are
$$
\int_0^1F(x)e(-kx)\,dx=\sum_n\int_n^{n+1}f(u)e(-ku)\,du=\widehat f(k).
$$
The series $\sum_k\widehat f(k)e(kx)$ converges absolutely and uniformly. Its <Fourier coefficients> equal those of $F$. The uniqueness theorem for <Fourier series> says that two <integrable functions> with the same coefficients agree almost everywhere; as both functions here are continuous, they agree everywhere. Evaluating at zero proves <Poisson summation>.

The <Gaussian Fourier transform> of $e^{-\pi tx^2}$, $t>0$, is $t^{-1/2}e^{-\pi\xi^2/t}$. Apply <Poisson summation> to obtain the transformation of the <Jacobi theta function>
$$
\vartheta(t)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}e^{-\pi n^2t},\qquad \vartheta(t)=t^{-1/2}\vartheta(1/t).
$$
For $\Re s>1$, termwise integration in the <Mellin transform> gives the <Mellin representation of the completed Riemann zeta function>
$$
\Lambda(s):=\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\zeta(s)=\frac12\int_0^\infty(\vartheta(t)-1)t^{s/2-1}\,dt.
$$
Split at one and substitute $t=1/u$ in the lower integral. The theta transformation gives
$$
\Lambda(s)=\frac1{s-1}-\frac1s+\frac12\int_1^\infty(\vartheta(t)-1)\bigl(t^{s/2-1}+t^{(1-s)/2-1}\bigr)\,dt.
$$
The integral is an <entire function> of $s$, since $\vartheta(t)-1$ decays exponentially. This <pole-subtracted theta integral for the completed zeta function> supplies a <meromorphic continuation> with simple <poles> at zero and one. Both the rational term and the integral are unchanged by $s\mapsto1-s$, proving the <functional equation of the Riemann zeta function>
$$
\boxed{\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\zeta(s)=\pi^{-(1-s)/2}\Gamma((1-s)/2)\zeta(1-s).}
$$
Equivalently the <Riemann xi function> $\xi(s)=\frac12s(s-1)\Lambda(s)$ is <entire> and satisfies $\xi(s)=\xi(1-s)$. The reflection and duplication identities for the <Gamma function> also give $\zeta(s)=2^s\pi^{s-1}\sin(\pi s/2)\Gamma(1-s)\zeta(1-s)$ by <meromorphic continuation>.

= 2
{parent=Paper 30}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Write $F(s)=-\zeta'(s)/\zeta(s)$ and $\psi_\Gamma=\Gamma'/\Gamma$. The <global partial-fraction expansion of the zeta logarithmic derivative> is
$$
F(s)=\frac1{s-1}-\frac12\log\pi+\frac12\psi_\Gamma(1+s/2)-B-\sum_\rho\left(\frac1{s-\rho}+\frac1\rho\right),\qquad B=\log2+\frac12\log\pi-1-\frac\gamma2.
$$
Here $\gamma$ is the <Euler-Mascheroni constant>, and the sum includes multiplicities of the <nontrivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function>. The paired terms are locally absolutely convergent away from their <poles>, by the <Riemann–von Mangoldt formula>. This identity follows by differentiating the genus-one <Hadamard factorization> of the <Riemann xi function> and using the <Gamma function recurrence>.

We first prove the classical <zero-free region of the Riemann zeta function>. The <Euler-product nonvanishing of the Riemann zeta function> and its <functional equation> place every nontrivial zero in the closed strip $0\le\beta\le1$; no assertion about zeros on its boundary is needed yet. The <real logarithmic derivative of Riemann xi> and the <digamma function> asymptotic imply, for $1<\sigma\le2$ and $|t|\ge1$,
$$
\Re F(\sigma+it)\le C\log(|t|+2)-\sum_\rho\frac{\sigma-\beta}{(\sigma-\beta)^2+(t-\gamma_\rho)^2}.
$$
Every summand being subtracted is nonnegative. If $\beta+it$ is a zero, retaining its summand gives $\Re F(\sigma+it)\le C\log(|t|+2)-1/(\sigma-\beta)$; at height $2t$ the upper bound $C\log(|t|+2)$ suffices. At real $\sigma$ the simple <pole> of $\zeta$ gives $F(\sigma)=1/(\sigma-1)+O(1)$.

For $\sigma>1$, the <Dirichlet series> $F(s)=\sum_n\Lambda(n)n^{-s}$ has nonnegative coefficients. The identity $3+4\cos u+\cos2u=2(1+\cos u)^2\ge0$ therefore gives the <three-four-one zero-free-region argument>
$$
0\le3F(\sigma)+4\Re F(\sigma+it)+\Re F(\sigma+2it).
$$
With $L=\log(|t|+2)$, a zero at $\beta+it$ would consequently satisfy
$$
\frac4{\sigma-\beta}\le\frac3{\sigma-1}+CL.
$$
Choose $\sigma=1+a/L$, with an absolute $a>0$ sufficiently small, and put $c=a/8$. If $\beta\ge1-c/L$, this inequality would imply $4/(a+c)\le3/a+C$. But $4/(a+a/8)-3/a=5/(9a)>C$ for small enough $a$, a contradiction.

For any fixed $t\ne0$, the same positivity inequality as $\sigma\downarrow1$ excludes a zero at $1+it$: its negative contribution has coefficient at least four, whereas the real <pole> contributes only three. Any zero at $1+2it$ only strengthens the contradiction. The <pole> at $s=1$ has a zero-free punctured neighbourhood. By compactness, the remaining bounded-height portion of the line has a zero-free neighbourhood as well. Shrink $c$ to combine these facts with the large-height bound. Hence
$$
\boxed{\zeta(\beta+i\gamma)\ne0\quad\text{if}\quad\beta\ge1-\frac c{\log(|\gamma|+2)},}
$$
with the understanding that $s=1$ is a <pole>.

The <Riemann–von Mangoldt explicit formula> for the <Second Chebyshev function>, with half weight at a prime-power endpoint, is
$$
\psi_0(x)=x-\lim_{T\to\infty}\sum_{|\Im\rho|\le T}\frac{x^\rho}{\rho}-\log(2\pi)-\frac12\log(1-x^{-2}),\qquad x>1.
$$
The zero sum uses symmetric height truncation. To estimate it, take a half-integer $x$ and use the <truncated explicit formula for the second Chebyshev function>
$$
\psi(x)=x-\sum_{|\Im\rho|\le T}\frac{x^\rho}{\rho}+O\left(\frac{x\log^2(xT)}T+\log x\right),\qquad 2\le T\le x.
$$
The zero-free region gives $|x^\rho|\le x\exp(-c\log x/\log(T+2))$ for these zeros. The <Riemann–von Mangoldt formula> and <partial summation> give $\sum_{|\Im\rho|\le T}|\rho|^{-1}\ll\log^2(T+2)$. Choose $T=\exp(\sqrt{\log x})$. Both the zero sum and the truncation error are then $O(xe^{-c'\sqrt{\log x}})$ after reducing the positive constant to absorb logarithmic factors. Replacing an arbitrary $X$ by $\lfloor X\rfloor+1/2$ preserves the <Von Mangoldt function> sum and changes the main term by at most one. This proves the requested form of the <Prime number theorem>:
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\le X}\Lambda(n)=X+O\left(Xe^{-c'\sqrt{\log X}}\right).}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 30}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Let $A(x)$ count <primes> $p\le x$ for which $p+2$ is also <prime>. We prove $A(x)\ll x/\log^2x$ using a quadratic <Selberg sieve>, then apply <Abel summation>.

For $F(n)=n(n+2)$, each odd <prime> has exactly two roots modulo that prime. The <Chinese remainder theorem> gives the <polynomial root density in a sieve>
$$
\#\{n\le x:d\mid F(n)\}=xg(d)+O(\rho_F(d)),\qquad g(d)=\frac{2^{\omega(d)}}d,
$$
for odd <squarefree integers> $d$. Put $R=x^{1/8}$ and choose the optimizing real <Selberg sieve weights> $\lambda_d$, supported on these $d\le R$, with $\lambda_1=1$. If $p,p+2$ are prime and $p>R$, their weight $(\sum_{d\mid F(p)}\lambda_d)^2$ is one. It is nonnegative for every other integer. Expanding the square therefore gives
$$
A(x)\le O(R)+x\sum_{d,e\le R}\lambda_d\lambda_e g([d,e])+O\left(\sum_{d,e\le R}|\lambda_d\lambda_e|\rho_F([d,e])\right).
$$
The <Selberg sieve diagonalization> makes the main quadratic form $1/J(R)$, where
$$
J(R)=\sum_{\substack{d\le R\\d\text{ odd and squarefree}}}\prod_{p\mid d}\frac2{p-2}.
$$
The <optimal Selberg weights have modulus at most one>. Since $\rho_F([d,e])\le[d,e]\le R^2$, the remainder is at most $O(R^4)$.

For completeness, the lower bound on $J$ is where the second logarithm enters. Restrict to <primes> $3\le p\le y=R^{1/8}$ and put $k(p)=2/(p-2)$. The full product $W=\prod_{3\le p\le y}(1+k(p))$ is $\asymp\log^2 y$, since its <logarithm> is $2\sum_{3\le p\le y}1/p+O(1)$ and the <Mertens second theorem> applies. Give the associated <squarefree divisor> $d$ probability $k(d)/W$. Prime inclusion probabilities are $2/p$, so the <Mertens first theorem> gives
$$
\mathbb E\log d=2\sum_{3\le p\le y}\frac{\log p}p=2\log y+O(1)<\frac12\log R
$$
for large $R$. The <Markov inequality> shows that at least half the weight has $d\le R$. This proves the <truncated Euler-product lower bound> $J(R)\gg W\gg\log^2R$. Consequently the <twin-prime upper bound from a quadratic sieve> is
$$
A(x)\ll R+\frac{x}{\log^2R}+R^4\ll\frac{x}{\log^2x}.
$$
Finally, <Abel summation> gives
$$
\sum_{\substack{p\le X\\p,p+2\text{ prime}}}\frac1p=\frac{A(X)}X+\int_2^X\frac{A(t)}{t^2}\,dt.
$$
The tail is dominated by $\int_3^\infty dt/(t\log^2t)<\infty$. Thus <reciprocal-sum convergence from a counting bound> proves convergence of the first reciprocal sum. The second is smaller termwise. We have proved <Brun's theorem>:
$$
\boxed{\sum_{p,p+2\text{ prime}}\left(\frac1p+\frac1{p+2}\right)<\infty.}
$$
Counting each twin prime just once changes at most a finite repetition and gives the same conclusion.

